  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday and Wednesday: The Inland Northwest will sit under an \namplifying upper-level ridge extending from the desert southwest \nwhile a closed low pressure system moves northward offshore the west \ncoast. Southerly flow aloft will maintain a steady stream of \nmonsoonal moisture into the region, resulting in periods of cloud \ncover. As the ridge amplifies, 850 mb temperatures are forecast to \nclimb to around 25C on Tuesday and 27-28C on Wednesday, supporting \nwidespread surface high temperatures in the upper 90s to lower 100s. \nThere is uncertainty in the temperature forecast due to the cloud \ncover and smoke from local fires in Washington and Oregon. Light \nwinds will allow smoke to accumulate near active fires, likely \nblocking afternoon solar radiation and locally limiting the degree \nof warming compared to the current forecast.\n\nThursday through Saturday: A critical fire weather pattern will \nevolve as the offshore upper low approaches the Pacific Northwest \nand embedded shortwaves move through the region. Continued monsoonal \nmoisture advection will result in elevated instability, bringing a \n10-20% chance of thunderstorms as early as late Wednesday night into \nThursday morning. Instability transitions to surface based by \nThursday afternoon, supporting a continued 12-25% chance of \nthunderstorms. Very hot conditions will persist into Thursday with \nmodel soundings showing DCAPE values exceeding 1000 J/kg in the \nafternoon. This will create a favorable environment for strong, \nlocalized downdraft wind gusts with any shower or thunderstorm \nactivity.\n\nBy Friday into Saturday, an elongated upper-level trough digging off \nthe Pacific Northwest coast will push the initial offshore low \neastward, driving a dry cold front across the region. An increasing \ncross-Cascade pressure gradient will bring breezy winds through the \nCascade gaps beginning Thursday, followed by widespread breezy \nconditions Friday and Saturday. The NBM indicates a 30-70% chance \nacross the Inland Northwest for sustained wind speeds exceeding 15 \nmph combined with minimum relative humidity below 20%. The timeline \nof very hot temperatures, lightning potential, and subsequent dry, \nbreezy frontal passages presents a high impact fire weather scenario \nfor the Inland Northwest Thursday through Saturday.\n\nSunday and Monday: Ensembles are in broad agreement on an offshore \nGulf of Alaska low late in the weekend, with southwest flow aloft \nacross the Pacific Northwest. Model spread remains high regarding \nthe trajectory of this system into early next week, with additional \nuncertainty in the forecast emerging. There is relatively high \nconfidence though for temperatures to gradually cool late in the \nweek into the weekend with high temperature decreasing into the 80s \nto lower 90s for Sunday and Monday. /vmt",
